## Track 4 — Optimize for Casual Investors
Strip the jargon everywhere. Every signal gets a plain English "what this means" summary at the top before any technical analysis. Reframe alerts as "stories to watch" not signals. Weekly digest email showing top 3 market stories of the week with their outcomes. The topology is our core differentiator here — casual investors want to understand why markets move, not just get a ticker and a direction. We are the tool that explains it visually.

> **Can run in parallel with Track 3 frontend work. No backend dependencies.**

### Subtasks

**4.1 — Plain English summary field**
- Add a `plain_english_summary` field to the AI signal prompt
- Prompt instruction: `"Write a 2-sentence plain English explanation of this signal as if explaining to someone who does not work in finance. No jargon, no ticker symbols, no financial terms."`
- Store in signals table
- Display at the very top of the Analysis Node panel above all other fields
- Label it: `"What's happening"` not `"Summary"` or `"AI Reasoning"`

**4.2 — Language audit across the entire UI**
- Go through every label, button, tooltip, and section header
- Replace: `Signal` → `Story`, `Bearish` → `Likely to fall`, `Bullish` → `Likely to rise`, `Time Horizon` → `When to watch`, `Confidence` → `How certain`
- Keep the technical terms available on hover/expand for users who want them
- Do not rename things in the database or API — frontend display only

**4.3 — Reframe Telegram alerts**
- Change Telegram message header from `BULLISH — NVDA, AAPL` to `📖 New story: [headline]`
- Lead with plain English summary, put tickers and direction below
- Add a `Why this matters` section using the `plain_english_summary` field
- Keep the full technical breakdown available but below the fold

**4.4 — Weekly digest**
- Every Sunday at 9am, send email digest to subscribed users
- Content: top 3 stories of the week by confidence score
- For each story: headline, plain English summary, tickers, and outcome (did the signal play out?)
- Outcome data comes from Track 1 performance tracking
- Use a simple transactional email service — Resend or Nodemailer
- **Gate: only build after Track 1 performance data exists so outcomes can be shown**

